 \begin{abstract}
 % BEGIN-EV2
 A buyer of computation often cannot see the quality of delivered work and can afford to check only a sample of it. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, and free identity replacement, in a mechanism class without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral before receipts; a failed audit refunds the job's escrow and bans the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, immediate free replacement makes the ban costless against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry comes before the next allocation. The rule we study, R3, checks every job of a new identity while the round's audits allow it, and after that window keeps targeting the newest identities, so a replacement can stay under heavy inspection: persistent age-ranked targeting rather than release by age. We report two registered tests. In the first, the original comparative claim failed; an amended claim, registered after development results were seen, passed on the public identity-replacement family. The second test compares R3 with a verified-history comparator C$^*$ at matched budget, payment, tolerance, ban, pacing, and sanctions, decomposes R3 into its three parts, adds stronger attacks, and repeats the B3--R3 comparison on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
-Pooled over the six fresh markets, the registered procedure classifies R3 and C$^*$ as ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_q1_est} under D2+H3 and \site{abs_q1_est_x} under X7-SC, almost all of it in the profit the attacker earns when it deviates. The registered intervals hold the six markets fixed; treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] under D2+H3 (exploratory), so neither the ordering nor the equivalence extends to the neighbourhood. The X7-SC search returned the D2+H3 winner it starts from in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} Q1 cells, so the two families are close to one test. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, not on attacker gain. R3's two targeting parts are substitutes (factorial contrast \site{abs_q2_pg}): once newest-first targeting is in place, the priority phase changes the gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}; pacing slightly raises the measured gain. At the headline market, the selected sealed-attacker policy gains \site{abs_q3_f111_h4} against R3; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{abs_q3_w0}, and similarly for B3 and C$^*$; and an exploratory lottery between a welfare attack and a profitable attack, selected on the evaluation records, has estimated normalised gain \site{abs_ph_mix_gain} and gross efficiency \site{abs_ph_mix_eff} against R3, with its population profitability not established. The B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of the six fresh markets, all of them qualified as results of the prescribed search procedure.
-All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references and are conditional on the sampled markets, the stated neighbourhood, the fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and a population whose honest providers never replace their identities.
