 \begin{abstract}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-A buyer of computation often cannot see the quality of delivered work and can afford to check only a sample of it. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, and free identity replacement, in a mechanism class without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral before receipts; a failed audit refunds the job's escrow and bans the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, immediate free replacement makes the ban costless against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry comes before the next allocation. The rule we study, R3, checks every job of a new identity while the round's audits allow it, and after that window keeps targeting the newest identities, so a replacement can stay under heavy inspection: persistent age-ranked targeting rather than release by age. We report two registered tests. In the first, the original comparative claim failed; an amended claim, registered after development results were seen, passed on the public identity-replacement family. The second test compares R3 with a verified-history comparator C$^*$ at matched budget, payment, tolerance, ban, pacing, and sanctions, decomposes R3 into its three parts, adds stronger attacks, and repeats the B3--R3 comparison on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
-Pooled over the six fresh markets, the registered procedure classifies R3 and C$^*$ as ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_q1_est} under D2+H3 and \site{abs_q1_est_x} under X7-SC, almost all of it in the profit the attacker earns when it deviates. The registered intervals hold the six markets fixed; treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] under D2+H3 (exploratory), so this analysis does not establish the ordering or the equivalence for the neighbourhood. The X7-SC search returned the D2+H3 winner it starts from in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} Q1 cells, so the two families are close to one test. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the runner-up left a lower development gain. R3's two targeting parts are substitutes (factorial contrast \site{abs_q2_pg}): once newest-first targeting is in place, the priority phase changes the gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}; pacing slightly raises the measured gain. At the headline market, the selected sealed-attacker policy gains \site{abs_q3_f111_h4} against R3, the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{abs_q3_w0}, and similarly for B3 and C$^*$, and the registered welfare search under a profitability constraint found no profitable attack against R3. The B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of the six fresh markets, all of them qualified as results of the prescribed search procedure.
-All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references and are conditional on the sampled markets, the stated neighbourhood, the fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities.
+A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral. For uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement, free replacement makes the ban costless against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and afterwards keeps targeting the newest identities. We report two registered tests; the second compares R3 with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
+We report four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}); treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}], so the ordering is not established beyond these markets, and the second attack family returned the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does almost all the work: once it is in place, R3's priority window changes the attacker's gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}. Third, the targeting also falls on honest newcomers: in a registered diagnostic at the headline market, R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of an honest replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, against \site{abs_hon_cs_1} under C$^*$. Fourth, under the prescribed D2+H3 searches, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules to about \site{abs_q3_w0}.
+All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references, six markets from one neighbourhood, fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
 % END-EV2
 \end{abstract}
 
