+Pooled over the six fresh markets, the registered procedure classifies R3 and C$^*$ as ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_q1_est} under D2+H3 and \site{abs_q1_est_x} under X7-SC, almost all of it in the profit the attacker earns when it deviates. The registered intervals hold the six markets fixed; treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] under D2+H3 (exploratory), so this analysis does not establish the ordering or the equivalence for the neighbourhood. The X7-SC search returned the D2+H3 winner it starts from in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} Q1 cells, so the two families are close to one test. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the runner-up left a lower development gain. R3's two targeting parts are substitutes (factorial contrast \site{abs_q2_pg}): once newest-first targeting is in place, the priority phase changes the gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}; pacing slightly raises the measured gain. At the headline market, the selected sealed-attacker policy gains \site{abs_q3_f111_h4} against R3, the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{abs_q3_w0}, and similarly for B3 and C$^*$, and the registered welfare search under a profitability constraint found no profitable attack against R3. The B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of the six fresh markets, all of them qualified as results of the prescribed search procedure.
+All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references and are conditional on the sampled markets, the stated neighbourhood, the fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities.
+\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) At the headline market, the registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against R3 (its selected policy has zero estimated gain), and an exploratory lottery between the unconstrained welfare attack and a profitable attack reaches substantially lower empirical gross efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; this follows from averaging the two components, and its population profitability remains unestablished. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
+\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Every identity is new at the start. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity belongs to a provider that has replaced, usually the focal one; a truthful focal provider may also replace after a false failure, so a new identity marks a replacement history, not dishonest delivery. Churn of honest identities is outside the model, except for the single replacement of the second test's honest-replacement diagnostic (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).
+\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective, the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin at the headline market and a development market; at every configuration the efficiency margin was the smaller one, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency; at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain, and in the full-budget rechecks the runner-up left a lower D2+H3 gain than C$^*$ (\const{0.076} against \const{0.097} at the headline market, \const{0.018} against \const{0.038} at dev0), so a comparator selected on attacker gain would likely have been the runner-up; the two best were rechecked at full budget (runner-up C-thr($K = \const{12}$)), and the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$). C$^*$ is the best of four tested adaptations; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.
+R3 audits every served job of identities younger than $L$ rounds (age counted from the first offer) whenever the round's audit count covers them. Under overload, jobs are audited by age group, newest first, with $k$ the round's available audit count; an identity that offers but wins no jobs still ages; within the focal identity's group of $n$ jobs, of which $s$ are its own, the number audited is hypergeometric with $k' = \min\{n, \max(0, k - H)\}$ draws, $H$ being the fresh jobs of strictly newer identities.
+\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a $t$ interval over the per-market differences is [\site{r_ml_lo}, \site{r_ml_hi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_pos} of six markets positive, and [\site{r_ml_xlo}, \site{r_ml_xhi}] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [\site{r_ml_dlo}, \site{r_ml_dhi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_dpos} positive. The D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, the attacker's pooled mean gain against C$^*$ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of \site{r_rel}. The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC's search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in \site{r_x7_same} of the \site{r_x7_cells} Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C$^*$ and fresh4 against R3 differ. With each rule's strongest training-selected family instead (Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope}), the pooled difference is \site{r_env_pool}, and \site{r_env_f1} on fresh1, where X5-AG/v2 is C$^*$'s strongest family. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain than C$^*$ (Section~\ref{sec:rules}); a comparator selected on attacker gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1}, and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns slightly less per round and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
+\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). R3's selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C$^*$ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records, and they show that the W1 policy class is not closed under randomisation. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the W1 policy class is not closed under randomisation; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
+\paragraph{Predictions.} Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} score every registered prediction from the report. For Q4-b, the original scoring script compared the report's raw component $p$-values, although the prediction is registered on calibrated values; the corrected script compares the calibrated values, \site{r_pred_q4b_g} against \site{r_pred_q4b_e} on each of fresh0, fresh2, and fresh5. The prediction remains fulfilled and the total is unchanged: a scoring correction with no change of verdict, separate from the corrected labels of Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4}. The original numerical scoring path, preserved in the released scoring output, retains estimates from completed searches even when their doubling checks were marked incomplete; it counts \site{r_pred_n_ful} predictions fulfilled and \site{r_pred_n_fail} failed. A separate eligibility analysis applies the two filters below. The registered scoring rule also says that an incomplete target, in the sense of the design's search-quality checks, is not assessed; those checks call a search-flagged cell incomplete while keeping its estimate for the prescribed search procedure; the rule does not say whether a prediction computed from a flagged search is itself incomplete. Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} adds two post hoc eligibility readings. Starting from the listed inputs of each scored comparison, we propagate flags through recorded upstream links, including references, attack banks, and warm starts. An inherited flag identifies a potentially relevant ancestor; it does not establish that the flagged candidate was selected or that the statistic would change. The map is released with the scorer, and neither sensitivity is designated as the official retrospective score. If a flagged listed input or recorded ancestor makes a Q3 policy comparison ineligible, Q3-a, Q3-b, Q3-d, and Q3-e are not assessed: \site{r_pred_q_ful} predictions are fulfilled, \site{r_pred_q_fail} fail, and \site{r_pred_q_na} are not assessed (counting direct inputs only, Q3-e stays fulfilled: \site{r_pred_qd_ful}, \site{r_pred_qd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_qd_na}). The broader sensitivity excludes flagged scored cells outside six decision-related predictions retained by convention: Q1-a, its alternative, Q1-b, Q1-d, Q4-a, and Q4-b (the design keeps the decisions but does not say how predictions about them are scored). A prediction still fails if any eligible cell fails; otherwise, an excluded required cell leaves it not assessed. Q1-c and Q2-a to Q2-d are then also not assessed: \site{r_pred_d_ful} fulfilled, \site{r_pred_d_fail} failed, and \site{r_pred_d_na} not assessed (counting direct inputs only: \site{r_pred_dd_ful}, \site{r_pred_dd_fail}, and \site{r_pred_dd_na}). No reading turns a failed prediction into a fulfilled one or changes a registered decision; the marked rows of Tables~\ref{tab:predictions} and~\ref{tab:predictions-b} are those a stricter reading changes, and their estimates stand as estimates of the prescribed searches. The predictions differ in stakes and are not independent (Q1-a, its alternative, and Q1-d are decided by the same Q1 categories, and Q3-e passes by less than one hundred-thousandth), so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score. The failures are informative: we expected the priority phase to matter little, the replication to hold on at least five markets, X5-AG/v2 to stay below D2+H3, and the unconstrained welfare attack to leave R3's and C$^*$'s efficiency at the registered level, and none of these held (the first is not assessed under the broader reading).
+\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity marks a provider that has replaced; population churn of honest identities is untested. With such churn, newest-first targeting would spend audits on honest newcomers and could lose much of its targeting power, and the registered honest-replacement diagnostic covers a single replacement only. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
+For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; across markets the difference varies in sign, and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search yields gross efficiency near \site{c_w1} for both rules. Exploratory episode-level mixtures selected on the evaluation records yield, with H4, estimated efficiencies of \site{c_mx_r3} for R3 and \site{c_mx_cs} for C$^*$; public D2+H3 mixtures meeting the empirical training constraint yield \site{c_mx_r3_p} and \site{c_mx_cs_p}, respectively; all at non-negative estimated evaluation gain. The registered W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies; the population profitability of the selected mixtures remains unestablished. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
+A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral. For uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement, free replacement makes the ban costless against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and afterwards keeps targeting the newest identities. We report two registered tests; the second compares R3 with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
+We report four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}); treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}], so the ordering is not established beyond these markets, and the second attack family returned the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does almost all the work: once it is in place, R3's priority window changes the attacker's gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}. Third, the targeting also falls on honest newcomers: in a registered diagnostic at the headline market, R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of an honest replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, against \site{abs_hon_cs_1} under C$^*$. Fourth, under the prescribed D2+H3 searches, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules to about \site{abs_q3_w0}.
+All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references, six markets from one neighbourhood, fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
+\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) In a registered diagnostic, R3 keeps auditing an honest provider's replacement identity well after its window, at a small cost to that provider: the targeting that works against the attacker also burdens honest newcomers. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
