Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
Two Registered Tests of an Audit Rule
Ömer Demirel
Draft 9, November 2026

Abstract
A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
can return at once under a new identity. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy
delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, without entry
fees, identity certification, or collateral. For uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement,
free replacement makes the ban costless against honest rivals whose play does not depend on
the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget
allows and afterwards keeps targeting the newest identities. We report two registered tests; the
second compares R3 with a verified-history comparator, C∗ , at matched budget and sanctions on
six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash. We report four findings.
First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C∗ are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a
pre-set margin of ±0.05 (C∗ minus R3: 0.021); treated as a sample of markets, the per-market
differences give a t interval of [−0.013, 0.054], so the ordering is not established beyond these
markets, and the second attack family returned the first family’s winner in 10 of 12 cells. Second,
newest-first targeting does almost all the work: once it is in place, R3’s priority window changes
the attacker’s gain by only −0.001. Third, the targeting also falls on honest newcomers: in a
registered diagnostic at the headline market, R3 audits 0.917 of an honest replacement identity’s
jobs in its second ten rounds, against 0.161 under C∗ . Fourth, under the prescribed D2+H3
searches, R3 lowers the attacker’s measured gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market,
but the registered replication passes on 3 of six; welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all
three core rules to about 0.840. All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references,
six markets from one neighbourhood, fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and
honest opponents that never replace their identities; C∗ was selected in development on gross
efficiency in effect.

1

Introduction

A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost
and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are
expensive and run after the response is served. FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed
integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the
response is served, at about 70 to 95 times the cost of one forward pass [27, p. 18], for a window of
1024 tokens per request, and the client checks each proof on a CPU [27, pp. 16–17]. Its Lean proofs
cover the protocol; the equivalence of the executable prover and verifier to it is not proved [27, p. 17].
Such proofs detect a substituted computation; they do not prevent an incorrect response from being
served. If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions become
which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.

1

